  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1250 PM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin Fri morning with GA positioned between a \nhigh pressure over the Mid-Atlantic and 2 low pressure centers with \none over the northeastern Gulf and the second developing just off \nthe South FL Atlantic coast. The ridge to the north is weakening a \nbit as its center moves NE Fri allowing moisture to continue to \nspread into south and central GA Georgia. Both the GFS and ECMWF \nkeep the deep layered moisture and better dynamics south of the area \nbut we could still see come isolated to scattered showers across \ncentral GA Fri and Sat. WPC has put east central GA under an \nExcessive Rain Outlook (ERO) for Fri and Sat. Precipitable water \nvalues are expected in the 1\" to 1.5\" range across central GA \nFri/Sat. \n\nDuring the day Fri the low center just off the S FL Atlantic coast \nbegins to develop becoming the dominant low center. This low is \nexpected to deepen fairly substantially through Sat morning as it \nmoves North to just east of the GA coast. Both the GFS and ECMWF \nshow this low center continuing to develop through the weekend as it \nmoves up the eastern seaboard. The models show minor differences in \ntiming and location but they both show it becoming a very vigorous \nstorm as it moves NE through day 7. Drier air on the back side of \nthis low and a mid-level ridge traversing the Southeast is \nanticipated to promote benign weather Sunday and Monday. For now the \nbiggest issue we see with this developing low center is the \ntightening pressure gradient between the low and the ridge will drive \nbreezy easterly winds across the forecast area Fri night and \nSaturday.\n\nLow level moisture and increased cloud cover will contribute to low \ntemperatures in the upper 40s to mid 50s each morning over the \nweekend and into the early parts of next week. These lows will be \nbetween 5 to 10 degrees above seasonal norms. Low temps do begin to \ncool to near normal by day 7. High temperatures will largely be in \nthe upper 60s to 70s each afternoon.",
